From be02a4929940ffb15585b8a68881ce0e29d23e71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:45:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] qualcommax: ipq807x: add support for Arista C-230

Hardware specification:
- SoC: Qualcomm IPQ8071A, 4x Cortex-A53 (AC02 reference design)
- RAM: 1 GiB DDR4
- Flash: 32 MiB SPI NOR (Macronix MX25U25635F),
  512 MiB NAND (Macronix MX30UF4G18AB)
- WLAN: IPQ8074 (5 GHz 4x4:4 with QCN5154, 2.4 GHz 2x2:2 with QCN5124);
  QCA9882 on PCIe 0 (2.4/5 GHz, 2x2 802.11ac, the stock firmware's
  multifunction radio)
- Ethernet: lan1 100M/1G/2.5G/5G (Aquantia AQR114C, USXGMII, PoE in),
  lan2 10/100/1000 (Qualcomm Atheros AR8033, SGMII)
- Bluetooth: CSR8811 on a UART (needs its PS keys loaded over BCSP; not set up here)
- TPM: Infineon SLB9670 (TPM 2.0), SPI
- USB: 1x USB 2.0 type A
- LEDs: power, lan1, lan2, 2.4 GHz, 5 GHz and multifunction radio, each
  green and orange, on two 74LV164 shift registers
- Button: reset (pinhole)
- UART: RJ-45 console port, RS-232 levels, 115200 8N1
- Power: 802.3at PoE on lan1, or 12 V DC

Flash layout: the stock firmware keeps two slots, NAND "rootfs" (slot 0) and
"rootfs_1" (slot 1), their kernels in SPI NOR (0:hlos, 0:hlos_1). OpenWrt takes
NAND slot 0 for UBI (kernel, rootfs, rootfs_data); slot 1 stays as a fallback.
Slot 0 may be the one the stock firmware runs (pri=0); it is replaced all the
same, and pri=1 makes slot 1 the fallback. The device tree appends ubi.mtd=rootfs
and root=/dev/ubiblock0_1, so the root is found whatever U-Boot passes.

Flash instructions (console needed):
1. Stop U-Boot: it waits 2 s and stops only for the string
   s6.D5r4Gnb89ZEWSW6Kq.
   Do not let the stock firmware boot meanwhile: its boot script saves the
   U-Boot environment on every boot.
2. Boot the initramfs from a TFTP server:
     setenv ipaddr <AP address>; setenv serverip <TFTP server>
     setenv fdt_high 0x4A3E0000
     tftpboot 0x60000000 openwrt-qualcommax-ipq807x-arista_c-230-initramfs-uImage.itb
     bootm 0x60000000#config@ac02
3. Copy the factory image to /tmp and write it to slot 0 ("rootfs", mtd0 in
   /proc/mtd):
     ubidetach -p /dev/mtd0; ubiformat /dev/mtd0 -y -f /tmp/factory.ubi
4. Set the boot command, in one environment write, and reboot:
     fw_setenv -s - <<'EOF'
     owrt_args console=ttyMSM0,115200n8 rootwait panic=10
     owrt_parts setenv mtdids nand0=nand0; setenv mtdparts mtdparts=nand0:0x10000000@0x0(ubi)
     owrt_load ubi part ubi && ubi read 0x44000000 kernel
     owrt_boot setenv fdt_high 0x4A3E0000; run owrt_parts owrt_load && bootm 0x44000000#config@ac02
     arista_unset setenv mtdids; setenv mtdparts; setenv fdt_high 0x4A400000
     arista_args if test ${pri} = 0; then setenv bootargs ${ba0}; else setenv bootargs ${ba1}; fi
     arista_boot run arista_unset arista_args ubootscript
     bootcmd setenv bootargs ${owrt_args}; run owrt_boot; run arista_boot
     pri 1
     EOF

The stock boot (bootipq) picks its kernel through the BOOTCONFIG partitions;
this boot command loads OpenWrt's kernel from UBI instead and falls back to the
stock boot, slot 1. The stock boot script sets bootargs only when the slot
changes and then saves the environment, so the fallback first puts the stock
bootargs, fdt_high and partition settings back.

Return to stock: fw_setenv bootcmd 'run ubootscript', then reboot.

MAC addresses:
- lan1: 0:art 0x0 (label, 30:86:2d:xx:xx:xx)
- lan2: 0:art 0x18 (label - 1)
- WLAN: from the calibration data

Both ports are in the LAN by default, with DHCP, as on the Arista C-260.
Each Ethernet LED is green while its port has a link, as Arista's stock
firmware lights it. The TPM is held in reset until gpio62 is driven high,
as the stock firmware does before loading its driver; a gpio-hog does it.

Tested on one unit: installed from the initramfs as above, boots from NAND;
lan1 at 2.5G, lan2 at 1G, the three radios found with their board data and
calibration, LEDs, button, USB and the TPM.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
